# Example 7: Evolution simulation - mutation + selection + drift
# Concept demo: adaptive evolution of a population under selection pressure
#
# This .helix file declares one evolution run via the #evolve bio-instruction annotation,
# seeded with the ancestor gene; the Python API comments below supplement with equivalent script calls.
#
# Parameters (based on real data):
# - E. coli mutation rate 2.2e-10 /nt/gen (Lee 2012)
# - transition:transversion = 2:1 (Stoltzfus 2009)
# - Effective population size Ne = 1.3e8 (E. coli)
# - Wright-Fisher model: mutation -> selection -> drift -> recombination

# Seed gene: initial ancestral sequence
#gene name=ancestor
ATG CTG CTG CTG CTG CTG CTG CTG CTG CTG TAA
#end

# Evolution instruction: evolve 100 generations targeting ancestor, with mutation rate 0.01
# The parser records it as BioInstruction(kind=evolve, target=ancestor, params=...)
#evolve target=ancestor generations=100 mutation_rate=0.01 population_size=1000

